INT. LIGHTHOUSE - STAIRS - NIGHT
[[day:1]]

A spiral of iron stairs, a hundred and twelve of them. IRIS, seventies, climbs without hurrying. Maeve and Toby follow the candle.

IRIS
Mind the ninth step. And the fortieth. And most of the ones in between.

MAEVE
Who are you?

IRIS
I keep the light.

MAEVE
Nobody keeps the light. The board let the keepers go twenty years ago.

IRIS
The board let the keepers go. I didn't go.

TOBY
Have you been up here the whole time?

IRIS
Where else would I be? Somebody has to wind the clockwork, and the clockwork has never once complained about my cooking.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE - LAMP ROOM - NIGHT
[[day:1]]

Glass on every side. The great lamp turns, slow as a planet. Along one wall, biscuit tins, dozens of them, stacked to the ceiling.

Toby prises the lid off one. It is full of letters. Envelopes, addressed in the same hand, not one of them stamped.

TOBY
There must be a thousand.

IRIS
Twenty years is a lot of Sundays.

MAEVE
Why didn't you post them?

IRIS
The post stopped coming out to the rock the week the keepers left. I wrote to complain. I had no stamps, so I couldn't post the complaint either.

TOBY
So you put one in a bottle.

IRIS
I put one in a bottle every high water. You're the first person who ever picked one up.

Maeve lifts an envelope from the tin and reads the address. She goes very still.

MAEVE
This is my address.

IRIS
It's our address, Maeve. It was mine before it was yours.

Toby looks from one of them to the other.

TOBY
Auntie Maeve. Is she...

MAEVE
My sister left the island twenty years ago. She said she was going to the mainland. She said she would write.

IRIS
I did write.

INT. POST OFFICE - MORNING
[[day:2]]

The tide is out. Sun through the window. The biscuit tins are stacked on the counter, and Owen is carrying in more.

OWEN
That's the last of them. The ferry never carried anything that heavy that didn't complain about it.

Maeve tears a strip of stamps from the sheet. Toby works the franking machine: thump, thump, thump.

TOBY
How many do we have to do?

MAEVE
All of them. They're all to me, but they go through the post. That's the rule.

The shop bell rings over the door. Iris, in her cardigan, blinking at the daylight.

IRIS
You're sorting them in order?

MAEVE
Oldest first. I'm going to read them in the order you meant me to.

IRIS
That will take you twenty years.

MAEVE
Then you'd better stay and tell me which ones I can skip.

Iris sits on the stool behind the counter, where she sat when they were girls. Toby hands her the last stamp.

TOBY
Do you want to do the first one?

IRIS
(pressing it down, carefully)
There. Now it's posted.

The radio murmurs on the shelf. Outside, the gulls.

RADIO (V.O.)
Malin, Hebrides. Fair. Good, becoming very good.

FADE OUT.

THE END
